data.content:
The lightest approach for .NET 8+ is **`TimeProvider`** — it's built into the BCL, requires no extra packages, and doesn't need a DI container.

### How it works

1. Accept `TimeProvider` as an optional constructor parameter (defaulting to `TimeProvider.System`).
2. Call `timeProvider.GetUtcNow()` instead of `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`.
3. In tests, pass `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing.FakeTimeProvider` (from the `Microsoft.Extensions.TimeProvider.Testing` NuGet package) to control time.

### Applied to your code

```csharp
namespace InvoiceLib;

public class InvoiceCalculator
{
    private readonly TimeProvider _timeProvider;

    public InvoiceCalculator(TimeProvider? timeProvider = null)
    {
        _timeProvider = timeProvider ?? TimeProvider.System;
    }

    public bool IsOverdue(DateTimeOffset dueDate)
        => _timeProvider.GetUtcNow() > dueDate;
}
```

### In tests

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing;

var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 15, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 10, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

// Advance time:
fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(30));
Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 2, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

### Why this is the lightest option

- `TimeProvider` ships in `System.Runtime` (.NET 8+) — zero production dependencies.
- The optional parameter means existing callers keep working unchanged.
- No interfaces to define, no DI container required.

